1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,350
The Curiosity Club.

2
00:00:02,350 --> 00:00:04,741
Episode 27.

3
00:00:04,741 --> 00:00:07,377
The Tiny Bakers in the Dough.

4
00:00:07,377 --> 00:00:10,095
Here is something to listen for.

5
00:00:10,095 --> 00:00:13,165
What made the dough expand while it rested?

6
00:00:13,165 --> 00:00:14,592
Ready?

7
00:00:14,592 --> 00:00:16,671
Let us find out.

8
00:00:16,671 --> 00:00:20,326
The club visits a bakery before opening time.

9
00:00:20,326 --> 00:00:24,673
The baker shows them a bowl of dough and marks its starting height.

10
00:00:24,673 --> 00:00:28,057
We will check it again later, she says.

11
00:00:28,057 --> 00:00:30,366
Pip stares at the bowl.

12
00:00:30,366 --> 00:00:33,369
I am ready to watch every millimeter.

13
00:00:33,369 --> 00:00:37,133
While they wait, the baker shows them ingredients.

14
00:00:37,133 --> 00:00:44,646
One is yeast, a living organism so small that an individual cell needs magnification to be seen clearly.

15
00:00:44,646 --> 00:00:47,622
Leo looks closely at the packet.

16
00:00:47,622 --> 00:00:50,774
Are there tiny workers with tiny hats?

17
00:00:50,774 --> 00:00:53,749
No hats, the baker says.

18
00:00:53,749 --> 00:00:56,372
But yeast does useful work.

19
00:00:56,372 --> 00:01:00,557
It uses sugars and releases carbon dioxide gas.

20
00:01:00,557 --> 00:01:04,293
The dough traps many gas bubbles and expands.

21
00:01:04,293 --> 00:01:08,668
When they return, the dough has risen above the original mark.

22
00:01:08,668 --> 00:01:13,247
It has grown without anyone adding more flour, Maya says.

23
00:01:13,247 --> 00:01:17,853
The baker explains that the trapped gas has increased its volume.

24
00:01:17,853 --> 00:01:22,445
She does not say that a larger dough ball must contain more flour.

25
00:01:22,445 --> 00:01:27,690
Later, she handles the hot oven while the children watch from the visitor area.

26
00:01:27,690 --> 00:01:30,923
The smell of baking bread fills the room.

27
00:01:30,923 --> 00:01:38,315
Pip creates a staff list: one baker, many yeast cells, and three extremely interested visitors.

28
00:01:38,315 --> 00:01:42,214
Leo asks whether all tiny living things are bad.

29
00:01:42,214 --> 00:01:44,755
No, says the baker.

30
00:01:44,755 --> 00:01:48,233
Microorganisms have many different roles.

31
00:01:48,233 --> 00:01:51,236
Some help us make familiar foods.

32
00:01:51,236 --> 00:01:56,168
Maya writes the day's headline: Small helpers, big bubbles.

33
00:01:56,168 --> 00:01:59,130
Leo adds: Excellent smell.

34
00:01:59,130 --> 00:02:02,024
Further taste research requested.

35
00:02:02,024 --> 00:02:04,932
Now, a little more to discover.

36
00:02:04,932 --> 00:02:08,396
Baker's yeast is a microscopic fungus.

37
00:02:08,396 --> 00:02:13,138
It can use sugars and release carbon dioxide through fermentation.

38
00:02:13,138 --> 00:02:17,404
Gas bubbles become trapped in dough, helping it rise.

39
00:02:17,404 --> 00:02:22,554
The dough's volume increases without a matching increase in the amount of flour.

40
00:02:22,554 --> 00:02:26,521
Baking later changes and sets the bread's structure.

41
00:02:26,521 --> 00:02:29,361
Let us hear the main idea again.

42
00:02:29,361 --> 00:02:33,057
Yeast helped the dough expand by producing gas.

43
00:02:33,057 --> 00:02:36,426
The height mark made the change easy to see.

44
00:02:36,426 --> 00:02:41,766
The club learned that some microorganisms play useful roles in making food.

45
00:02:41,766 --> 00:02:44,741
Here are three useful expressions.

46
00:02:44,741 --> 00:02:47,866
You can listen, or say them with me.

47
00:02:47,866 --> 00:02:50,054
How much has it grown?

48
00:02:50,054 --> 00:02:52,241
How much has it grown?

49
00:02:52,241 --> 00:02:55,230
Let us compare it with the first mark.

50
00:02:55,230 --> 00:02:58,220
Let us compare it with the first mark.

51
00:02:58,220 --> 00:03:01,345
Tiny things can make a big difference.

52
00:03:01,345 --> 00:03:04,470
Tiny things can make a big difference.

53
00:03:04,470 --> 00:03:07,065
Remember our listening question?

54
00:03:07,065 --> 00:03:10,135
What made the dough expand while it rested?

55
00:03:10,135 --> 00:03:12,269
Here is the answer.

56
00:03:12,269 --> 00:03:17,785
Carbon dioxide produced by yeast became trapped as gas bubbles in the dough.

57
00:03:17,785 --> 00:03:21,440
And here is a question just for your imagination.

58
00:03:21,440 --> 00:03:27,065
What other useful things might be happening around you on a scale too small to see directly?

59
00:03:27,065 --> 00:03:30,176
There is more than one possible answer.

60
00:03:30,176 --> 00:03:33,423
Keep wondering, and see you next time.
